<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/expert/developing-a-personal-style">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Personal style is <strong>the residue of ten thousand decisions</strong> — not a discovery but
    a distillation. This lesson is the ladder\'s synthesis: everything above (fundamentals, media,
    subjects) plus time and self-honesty, reduced.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="deviations, constraints, volume, the market" widget="equationStep" config={styleCfg} />

  <h2>Practices that compound</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The sustained project:</strong> 50 portraits of the same street, 100 skies, one still life painted for a year — repetition under variation is the experimental design of style (the statistics lesson\'s controlled comparison, self-administered).</li>
    <li><strong>Your archive is data:</strong> lay out everything from the last 3 years — the through-line you can\'t see day-to-day is visible on the floor. The critique lesson\'s external eye, turned retrospective.</li>
    <li><strong>Steal like an artist, then be stolen from:</strong> influence chains (Degas → Toulouse-Lautrec → Picasso) show style as conversation — copying ONE master is imitation; synthesising five is a position.</li>
    <li><strong>Beware premature style:</strong> a signature look built before fundamentals calcifies into a gimmick — the reason serious teachers delay "find your style" until the craft can survive it (the sequence every ladder in this atlas shares: learn, then choose).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Austin Kleon, Steal Like an Artist; Bayles & Orland, Art & Fear; the design lesson\'s craft principles.</p>
</TopicPage>
